  "Source": "G. Hass, \"Preparation, properties and optical applications of thin films of titanium dioxide,\" Vacuum 2(4), 331-345 (1952). (doi not printed in the PDF) - Fig. 9 caption p. 338 and text p. 339 ('The first one on glass is silicon monoxide, having a thickness of half a wavelength and the other two are magnesium fluoride and titanium dioxide with nt = lambda/4 at lambda = 500 millimicrons')",
  "Notes": "Achromatic three-layer beam divider: half-wave SiO, quarter-wave MgF2 and quarter-wave TiO2 at 500 nm\nSpecification: Reflectance more than 50 % throughout the visible.\nReported: Text p. 339: R more than 50 % throughout the visible spectrum. Fig. 9 (plot only, approximate): R about 47-57 % with maxima near 450 and 650 nm and a shallow dip near 500 nm, falling to ~43 % at 800 nm; T about 40-50 %.\nNotes: Paper lists from the glass outward (SiO on glass); reversed here. Fig. 9 caption says 'lambda/2 of SiO, lambda/4 of MgF2 and lambda/4 of rutile (at lambda = 500 mu)'; the half-wave SiO is taken at 500 nm as well. SiO n = 1.97 (true SiO, nD) is an assumption; the actual SiO index may have been lower.\nLayers from Fig. 9 caption p. 338 and text p. 339 ('The first one on glass is silicon monoxide, having a thickness of half a wavelength and the other two are magnesium fluoride and titanium dioxide with nt = lambda/4 at lambda = 500 millimicrons'); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R >= 50.0 % from 400 to 700 nm; paper R > 50 % across the visible.",
